## §3.2 Mutation Frequency Calculation

| Value | Obtained | Published | Classification |
|---|---|---|---|
| Control MFMin mean | 17.4 | 17.4 | Exact |
| Low MFMin mean | 34.3 | 34.3 | Exact |
| Medium MFMin mean | 68.4 | 68.4 |  Exact |
| High MFMin mean | 95.4 | 95.4 |  Exact |
| Control MFMax mean | 21.7 | 21.7 |  Exact |
| Low MFMax mean | 51.5 | 51.5 |  Exact |
| Medium MFMax mean | 104.6 | 105.0 |  Rounding (104.6 → 105) |
| High MFMax mean | 262.8 | 263.0 |  Rounding |
| Low MFMin SEM | 1.38 | 1.38 |  Exact |
| Medium MFMin SEM | 5.72 | 5.72 |  Exact |
| High MFMin SEM | 2.87 | 2.87 |  Exact |
| Control MFMax SEM | 2.26 | 2.26 |  Exact |
| **Control MFMin SEM** | **1.29** | **2.26** | **⚠️ Paper typo** |

**Control MFMin SEM — paper typo identified:** The paper reports identical
SEM values (2.26) for both Control MFMin and Control MFMax. Two different
counting methods producing identical SEMs from the same six animals is
statistically implausible. Our analysis gives MFMin SEM = 1.29 and MFMax
SEM = 2.26. The MFMax SEM reproduces exactly (2.256878 → 2.26), confirming
our data is correct. The published MFMin SEM of 2.26 appears to be a
copy-paste error where the MFMax value was repeated.

**calculate_mf() grouping bug:** When `cols_to_group` includes both
`new_sample_id` and `dose_group`, the function produces 96 rows (24 samples
× 4 dose groups) rather than 24 rows, filling non-matching combinations with
zeros. Filtering to rows where `dose != NA` corrects this. This is a known
behaviour documented in the function warning about NA values in retained
metadata columns.

