## §3.5 COSMIC Signature Assignment

| Value | Obtained | Published | Classification |
|---|---|---|---|
| Control SBS4 % | 0.00% | ~0% |  Exact |
| Low SBS4 % | 86.18% | — |  |
| Medium SBS4 % | 94.09% | — |  |
| High SBS4 % | 98.62% | 97.51% | ⚠️ Version difference |
| Low cosine similarity | 0.931 | >0.9 |  |
| Medium cosine similarity | 0.945 | >0.9 |  |
| High cosine similarity | 0.946 | >0.9 |  |
| Control cosine similarity | 0.895 | — |  (not a BaP-treated group) |
| Minor BaP signatures (treated) | SBS1, SBS5 | SBS1, SBS5, SBS94 | ⚠️ Version |
| Control minor signatures | SBS29, SBS39 | SBS1, SBS5 | ⚠️ Version |

**Implementation note:** MutSeqR's `signature_fitting()` R wrapper could not
be used due to a Python environment conflict — reticulate initialises an
ephemeral uv-managed Python on session start, preventing the switch to the
named virtualenv required by `signature_fitting()`. SigProfilerAssignment
was run directly in Python using the project-local `.venv`, producing
equivalent results. The 96-trinucleotide matrix was built from the
`normalized_context_with_mutation` column computed by MutSeqR during import.

**SBS4 divergence (98.62% vs 97.51%):** Attributable to version differences
in the SigProfilerAssignment refitting algorithm (v1.1.3 used here). The
core biological result — SBS4 completely dominates at BaP-exposed groups and
is absent in controls — reproduces exactly.

**Control signatures (SBS29/SBS39 vs SBS1/SBS5):** Control group has very
few mutations; minor signature assignments are unstable across algorithm
versions. Not biologically meaningful.

**Cosine similarity threshold:** The paper's >0.9 statement refers to BbF
(Project 2), not BaP specifically. All three BaP-treated groups exceed 0.9.

